        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p64-q29-b-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "The histogram heights are ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "1,\\ 2,\\ \\frac13",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", matching the derivative on the three class intervals.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p64-q29-b-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Each height is the class frequency divided by its interval width.",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "The class frequencies are the successive increases in cumulative frequency: $1-0=1$, $5-1=4$ and $6-5=1$. A frequency histogram has height equal to frequency density.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Height on }[1,2)&=\\frac{1}{2-1}=1,\\\\\\text{Height on }[2,4)&=\\frac{4}{4-2}=2,\\\\\\text{Height on }[4,7)&=\\frac{1}{7-4}=\\frac13.\\end{align*}$$\nThese are exactly the derivative heights in part (a), so the horizontal derivative segments trace the tops of the histogram bars.",
          "solutionDiagrams": [
            {
              "id": "p64-q29-b-solution-histogram",
              "role": "solution",
              "format": "tikz",
              "code": "% mathsmap-diagram-colours {\"version\":1,\"kind\":\"graph\",\"base\":[],\"semantic\":[],\"reference\":\"PDF64 p64-q29-b; teacher PDF81 printed659\"}\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=0.65cm,y=1.45cm,font=\\fontsize{10}{12}\\selectfont]\n\\special{dvisvgm:raw <metadata data-graph-strokes=\"1\"/>}\n\\path[use as bounding box] (-0.5,-0.45) rectangle (8.6,2.65);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (1,0) rectangle (2,1);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (2,0) rectangle (4,2);\n\\draw[black,line width=0.8pt] (4,0) rectangle (7,{1/3});\n\\draw[black,->,line width=0.5pt] (0.5,0)--(8.4,0);\n\\draw[black,->,line width=0.5pt] (0.5,0)--(0.5,2.5);\n\\foreach \\x in {1,2,4,7}{\\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (\\x,0)--(\\x,0.06);\\node[below] at (\\x,0) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\x$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};}\n\\foreach \\y/\\lab in {0.5/0.5,1/1.0,1.5/1.5,2/2.0}{\\draw[black,line width=0.4pt] (0.5,\\y)--(0.62,\\y);\\node[left] at (0.5,\\y) {\\special{dvisvgm:raw <g data-graph-text=\"tick\">}$\\lab$\\special{dvisvgm:raw </g>}};}\n\\node[below] at (8.2,0) {$X$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}",
              "src": null,
              "widthMm": 72,
              "alt": "Mathematical diagram",
              "overlayOf": null,
              "transparent": false,
              "axes": null,
